WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A courier must visit a kiosk and then a garden.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:03.400 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Knowing the final location is useful, but the courier also
needs a route that avoids confusion at every turn.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:16.205 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we will track positions step by step and distinguish
the destination from the distance actually travelled.

00:00:16.205 --> 00:00:16.605 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to follow and describe routes using
coordinates and direction.

00:00:16.605 --> 00:00:28.725 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will move from objects or hands-on actions to drawings,
then connect those drawings with precise mathematical
descriptions.

00:00:28.725 --> 00:00:30.085 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep paper nearby if you would like to test an idea for
yourself.

00:00:30.085 --> 00:00:35.500 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can pause the player whenever you need more time.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.380 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to follow and describe routes using
coordinates and direction.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.180 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want to explain why a result is correct, as well as
obtain the result.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:50.571 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you watch, look for the evidence that would help you
convince a partner who has not seen your working.

00:00:50.571 --> 00:00:53.251 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Here are our three success criteria.

00:00:53.251 --> 00:00:55.211 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can track each route step.

00:00:55.211 --> 00:00:57.611 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can update the correct coordinate.

00:00:57.611 --> 00:01:00.371 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can check a destination and distance.

00:01:00.371 --> 00:01:04.251 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will check these ideas in two worked examples,
investigate a common mistake and finish with an independent
challenge.

00:01:04.251 --> 00:01:12.952 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A good explanation links what you see in the model with the
mathematical rule being used.

00:01:12.952 --> 00:01:16.472 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Put a counter at an intersection on squared paper.

00:01:16.472 --> 00:01:19.712 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Move it along grid lines, one interval at a time.

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:22.592 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Say right, left, up or down as you move.

00:01:22.592 --> 00:01:28.548 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Record a new ordered pair after each part of the journey,
instead of waiting until the very end.

00:01:28.548 --> 00:01:31.708 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A move right increases the first coordinate.

00:01:31.708 --> 00:01:33.588 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A move left decreases it.

00:01:33.588 --> 00:01:38.748 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The second coordinate stays unchanged because the counter
has not moved up or down.

00:01:38.748 --> 00:01:41.348 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Point to the row before and after moving.

00:01:41.348 --> 00:01:45.431 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both positions lie on the same horizontal line of the map.

00:01:45.431 --> 00:01:48.711 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A move upward increases the second coordinate.

00:01:48.711 --> 00:01:50.871 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A move downward decreases it.

00:01:50.871 --> 00:01:53.431 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The first coordinate stays unchanged.

00:01:53.431 --> 00:01:56.511 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check the column containing both points to see why.

00:01:56.511 --> 00:02:04.643 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These rules describe our map directions; a rotated map would
need its directions clarified before someone followed your
instructions.

00:02:04.643 --> 00:02:06.083 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Start at two, two.

00:02:06.083 --> 00:02:09.163 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Move four units right and then three units up.

00:02:09.163 --> 00:02:10.963 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the destination.

00:02:10.963 --> 00:02:14.963 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep an intermediate position for the end of the rightward
move.

00:02:14.963 --> 00:02:21.194 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That extra record will help you discover which step needs
fixing if your answer differs from your partner’s.

00:02:26.194 --> 00:02:29.554 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Moving four right changes two, two to six, two.

00:02:29.554 --> 00:02:33.034 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Moving three up then changes six, two to six, five.

00:02:33.034 --> 00:02:34.995 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The kiosk is at six, five.

00:02:34.995 --> 00:02:41.914 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The first part affected only the horizontal coordinate, and
the second affected only the vertical coordinate.

00:02:41.914 --> 00:02:43.964 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each step agrees with the grid.

00:02:43.964 --> 00:02:44.164 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
From the kiosk at six, five, travel two units left.

00:02:44.164 --> 00:02:47.564 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The first coordinate decreases from six to four while the
second stays five.

00:02:47.564 --> 00:02:54.044 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The garden is at four, five.

00:02:54.044 --> 00:02:59.164 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Starting this new step from the original starting point
would describe a different journey.

00:02:59.164 --> 00:03:02.404 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A learner starts every instruction from the origin.

00:03:02.404 --> 00:03:07.244 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That can find several separate points, but it does not
follow one continuous route.

00:03:07.244 --> 00:03:10.804 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each new movement starts where the previous movement ended.

00:03:10.804 --> 00:03:16.328 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Draw connected arrows and keep the most recent ordered pair
visible to avoid this mistake.

00:03:16.328 --> 00:03:20.568 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The courier travelled four units right, three units up and
two units left.

00:03:20.568 --> 00:03:23.968 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Add those distances to obtain nine units travelled.

00:03:23.968 --> 00:03:27.288 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The destination alone does not show that total.

00:03:27.288 --> 00:03:34.078 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Returning partway along a direction still adds distance to
the route, even when it reduces the first coordinate.

00:03:34.078 --> 00:03:37.838 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The courier is at four, five and wants to return to two,
two.

00:03:37.838 --> 00:03:42.078 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict one route using only horizontal and vertical moves.

00:03:42.078 --> 00:03:45.478 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Decide how much each coordinate must decrease.

00:03:45.478 --> 00:03:52.299 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
More than one order of instructions may work, so prepare to
justify yours using the actual starting point.

00:04:00.779 --> 00:04:01.139 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Move two units left from four, five to two, five.

00:04:01.139 --> 00:04:02.819 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then move three units down to two, two.

00:04:02.819 --> 00:04:05.619 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This return route has length five units.

00:04:05.619 --> 00:04:13.019 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Moving down first and left second would also reach home on
this unobstructed grid, but the intermediate point would be
different.

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:15.659 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A destination is one location.

00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:16.499 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A route is a sequence of movements leading there.

00:04:16.499 --> 00:04:23.179 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A detour can increase the total distance without changing
the destination.

00:04:23.179 --> 00:04:31.406 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
When comparing two plans, check both the ordered pairs and
the distances rather than assuming every path to the same
point is equally long.

00:04:31.406 --> 00:04:37.966 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our first-quadrant map does not show positions left of the
vertical axis or below the horizontal axis.

00:04:37.966 --> 00:04:40.246 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check a planned move before taking it.

00:04:40.246 --> 00:04:45.566 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If it would leave the displayed region, revise the
instructions or explain the limitation.

00:04:45.566 --> 00:04:49.141 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Do not silently wrap a counter to the opposite side.

00:04:49.141 --> 00:04:54.581 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Tell your partner the starting pair, each direction and
distance, and the intended destination.

00:04:54.581 --> 00:04:58.021 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ask the partner to draw the route without seeing your
version.

00:04:58.021 --> 00:05:02.021 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Comparing the two drawings is a useful test of
communication.

00:05:02.021 --> 00:05:06.150 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If they differ, identify the first instruction where the
paths separate.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:09.510 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A route goes three right and then three left from two, two.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:12.870 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A student says nothing happened because the destination is
unchanged.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:16.230 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Is that a complete description?

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:23.125 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Decide the destination and total distance separately, and
explain what the counter actually did during the journey.

00:05:28.125 --> 00:05:33.325 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The counter returns to two, two, but it travelled six units
altogether: three out and three back.

00:05:33.325 --> 00:05:37.365 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Zero change in position does not mean zero distance
travelled.

00:05:37.365 --> 00:05:42.951 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Recording the intermediate point five, two makes the
movement visible and supports the explanation.

00:05:42.951 --> 00:05:44.751 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to our learning goal.

00:05:44.751 --> 00:05:48.631 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Follow and describe routes using coordinates and direction.

00:05:48.631 --> 00:05:53.631 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one example from today and explain the evidence that
made its answer convincing.

00:05:53.631 --> 00:05:58.271 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which of our three success criteria feels secure, and which
needs another try?

00:05:58.271 --> 00:06:04.809 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Revisit a model, test your explanation with a partner, and
describe one mistake you can now notice and correct.
